<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Modern distributed ML suffers from a fundamental gap between the theoretical and realized performance of collective communication algorithms due to congestion and hop-count induced dilation in practical GPU clusters.
We present <span id="abstract1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span>, a Photonic Collective Communication Library that reconfigures the network topology to match the communication patterns of collective algorithms, thereby eliminating congestion and dilation by creating direct, contention-free circuits between communicating GPUs.
Unlike prior approaches that synthesize algorithms for specific network topologies and collectives, <span id="abstract1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> generalizes to any collective primitive and any topology by adapting the network to match each algorithm’s communication pattern.
<span id="abstract1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span>’s key innovation lies in its hardware-agnostic optimization framework that intelligently decides when to reconfigure based on the trade-off between network reconfiguration delay and congestion/dilation costs, making it practical across different optical hardware with varying switching speeds.
Our evaluation demonstrates that PCCL achieves up to 3<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> speedup over state-of-the-art algorithms on 128 GPUs across various workloads, buffer sizes, and topologies, translating to a <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.3\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.3\times</annotation></semantics></math> speedup in end-to-end training throughput.</span></p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Modern machine learning (ML) models are too large to fit in the memory of individual GPUs, causing training and inference to be distributed across multiple GPUs. Distributed ML workloads rely on collective communication primitives — for example, GPUs aggregate gradients via the <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> primitive during training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dongarra et al., 2013</a>)</cite>. These primitives are implemented by collective communication libraries (CCLs), like Nvidia’s Collective Communication Library or NCCL, in the ML software stack <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">NVIDIA NCCL, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Advanced Micro Devices, Inc., 2025</a>)</cite>. The efficiency of distributed ML depends on the performance of collective communication among GPUs. Inefficient collective communication leads to GPUs idling while waiting for data or parameters to arrive over the network <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">swi, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gebara et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lao et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Devlin et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decades of research on collectives.</span> 
The design of efficient algorithms for collective communication has been an active area of research in high-performance computing (HPC) for several decades <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dongarra et al., 2013</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thakur et al., 2005</a>)</cite>. Even with decades of progress in developing collective algorithms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Banikazemi et al., 1998</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhat and Prasanna, 2003</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lowekamp and Beguelin, 1996</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al., 2015</a>)</cite>, the gap between <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">theory</em> and <em id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">practice</em> remains stubborn: algorithms that are optimal in theory, routinely underperform on practical GPU deployments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shah et al., 2023</a>)</cite> since these algorithms disregard runtime properties of the underlying topology, including bandwidth variations due to <em id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">congestion</em> and latency variations due to high hop count or <em id="S1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dilation</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Leighton et al., 1994</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rothvoss, 2012a</a>)</cite>. This gap has led researchers to synthesize collective algorithms custom-fit to the underlying interconnect topology <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shah et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Won et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cai et al., 2021a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rashidi et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. However, synthesis is computationally intractable and does not scale to larger GPU deployments, leading to the use of heuristics that trade off optimality for synthesis time <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shah et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Won et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15450v1/motivation.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/135;" width="381" height="135"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>. </span><span id="S1.F1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance of the popular 3D Bucket algorithm for <span id="S1.F1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> collective and Hypercube algorithm for <span id="S1.F1.5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllToAll</span> collective on a 3D torus topology of size <math id="S1.F1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4\times 4\times 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\times 4\times 4</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jouppi et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Note that 3D bucket <span id="S1.F1.5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> algorithm designed by related perform well on 3D torus and Hypercube is a logarithmic latency algorithm for <span id="S1.F1.5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllToAll</span>. <span id="S1.F1.5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> matches the performance of 3D torus algorithms for <span id="S1.F1.5.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> and is <math id="S1.F1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="7.5\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7.5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7.5\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster than 3D Torus for <span id="S1.F1.5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllToAll</span> because it does not incur congestion and minimal reconfiguration delay.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Collective-aware topologies, not topology-aware collectives.</span> 
We propose a radically different alternative: instead of adapting the algorithm to a fixed network <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shah et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Won et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, we adapt the topology to match the communication pattern of the chosen collective algorithm. We present the <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Photonic Collective Communication Library</em> (<span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span>), which reconfigures the network topology to match the communication patterns of known optimal collective algorithms. This ability to reconfigure the network topology is made possible by the adoption of programmable optical fabrics in datacenters for ML <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jouppi et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahni et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu and Bergman, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. In each communication round of the algorithm, <span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> can implement direct, contention-free links between exactly the peers that communicate in that round. This eliminates sources of deviation between theory and practice of collective communication algorithms, namely congestion and dilation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Leighton et al., 1994</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rothvoss, 2012a</a>)</cite>. Without congestion and dilation, we can reuse decades of work on theoretically optimal collectives (<em id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> recursive halving/doubling or RHD, ring) and realize their textbook performance in practice.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Why is topology reconfiguration key?</span> 
Modern ML workloads expose the fundamental limitation of fixed or direct-connect network topologies: no single collective algorithm can efficiently support the diverse communication patterns within a training iteration. ML workloads invoke thousands of collectives with buffer sizes ranging from kilobytes to gigabytes, each requiring different algorithms for optimal performance. The ring algorithm excels for <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> with large buffers but incurs high latency costs for small buffers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shah et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">NVIDIA NCCL, 2021</a>)</cite>, while RHD reduces latency but causes congestion on direct-connect topologies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sensi et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. This mismatch is particularly acute in mixture-of-experts models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shazeer et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, which alternate between latency-sensitive <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllToAll</span> operations requiring low-diameter topologies and bandwidth-hungry <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> operations that favor ring-like high diameter topologies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>. We show that fixed topologies can force suboptimal performance for different collective algorithms degrading their communication time by 2-4<math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> due to congestion and dilation. <span id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> eliminates this constraint through topology reconfiguration, adapting the network to match each algorithm’s communication pattern. Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows how this enables using the theoretically optimal algorithm for every scenario—ring for large <span id="S1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> and specialized patterns for <span id="S1.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllToAll</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hardware architecture.</span> 
<span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> operates within a datacenter rack-scale GPU deployment, also known as the <em id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">scale-up domain</em>. Electrical switched scale-up domains (<em id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> NVSwitch) suffer from scaling challenges due to limited switch radix and high power consumption <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ikeda et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">HotChips 34, 5 26</a>)</cite>. To deliver high in-rack bandwidth while keeping energy use in check, ML datacenters are increasingly adopting photonic connectivity within the rack, giving rise to <em id="S1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">photonic GPU scale-up domains</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahni et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">HotChips 34, 5 26</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu and Bergman, 2025</a>)</cite>. Many architectures have been demonstrated to realize photonic scale-up domains in both industry and academia <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahni et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">HotChips 34, 5 26</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin et al., 2013</a>)</cite>. While some designs use co-packaged optical transponders <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Corporation, [n. d.]</a>)</cite> to connect GPU servers to a switch, recent work has demonstrated the viability of integrating chip-to-chip photonic connectivity directly into the server board using <em id="S1.p5.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">optical interposers</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">HotChips 34, 5 26</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahni et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt the interposer-based design as our evaluation platform—not because it is the only viable approach, but because it is an experimentally validated instantiation of a reconfigurable photonic scale-up fabric that enables us to demonstrate <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span>’s benefits with realistic hardware parameters (§<a href="#S2" title="2. Photonic scale-up GPU domains ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). While direct-connect topologies like 3D Torus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jouppi et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, 2D mesh also provide high in-rack bandwidth at a lower power consumption, these topologies are often optimized only for the <span id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> collective and result in suboptimal performance for other communication patterns like <span id="S1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllToAll</span>, as we show in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Technical Challenges.</span> 
Realizing <span id="S1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span>’s vision of topology-adaptive collective communication requires solving three interconnected challenges: (1) <span id="S1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> must determine <em id="S1.p7.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">when</em> reconfiguration is beneficial. Each topology change incurs a hardware-dependent delay, and <span id="S1.p7.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> must decide whether this cost is outweighed by the congestion and dilation savings for the remaining communication rounds. (2) <span id="S1.p7.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> must efficiently map collective algorithms to physical circuits. Given a collective algorithm’s communication pattern for each round, <span id="S1.p7.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> must construct conflict-free optical paths between communicating GPU pairs while respecting hardware constraints: each GPU has limited transmitters and receivers, and each optical waveguide can carry only one circuit per wavelength. (3) <span id="S1.p7.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> must scale the entire optimization without exponential search time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL<span id="S1.p8.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">’s contributions.</span></span> 
We address these technical challenges in <span id="S1.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span>, a collective communication library that bridges the theory-practice gap in collective communication by dynamically reconfiguring network topology to match collective algorithms’ communication patterns (§<a href="#S3" title="3. Congestion + dilation in collectives ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). Unlike prior work that synthesizes new algorithms for fixed topologies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shah et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Won et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, <span id="S1.p8.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> takes existing, well-understood algorithms and ensures they achieve their theoretical performance (§<a href="#S4" title="4. Circuit-switched collectives ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). <span id="S1.p8.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> makes the following contributions:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Practical performance through intelligent reconfiguration scheduling.</span>  <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> reduces the reconfiguration decision space from exponential to <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\log N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\log N)</annotation></semantics></math> binary choices by having the collective algorithm dictate the topology for each round, leaving only <em id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">when</em> to reconfigure as the optimization problem. <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span>’s optimization can be solved in less than one second for the largest scale-up domains.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithmic flexibility across diverse scenarios.</span> 
With microsecond-scale optical reconfiguration, the cost of per-round topology adaptation is far outweighed by achieving consistent theoretical performance across all collective primitives, buffer sizes, and parallelization strategies. This flexibility transforms <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> from a point optimization into a general solution that works with any initial topology (ring, torus, hypercube) and recovers the theoretical performance of any collective algorithm.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span> 
We show through extensive experiments that <span id="S1.p9.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span>’s <span id="S1.p9.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> outperforms or matches the performance of well-known optimal algorithms regardless of the initial underlying accelerator topology despite incurring reconfiguration delays. <span id="S1.p9.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span>’s <span id="S1.p9.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllToAll</span> algorithm outperforms existing well-known algorithms regardless of the initial underlying accelerator topology. Our evaluation shows that <span id="S1.p9.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> achieves up to <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2.5\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.5\times</annotation></semantics></math> speedup over state-of-the-art <span id="S1.p9.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> algorithms on 128 GPUs, with consistent improvements across different collective primitives, buffer sizes, and scale-up domain sizes. These improvements translate to <math id="S1.p9.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.3\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.3\times</annotation></semantics></math> speedup in end-to-end training of popular ML models (§<a href="#S6" title="6. End-to-end evaluation ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Differentiating from work on optical reconfiguration.</span> 
Recent work has proposed leveraging optical reconfiguration in ML datacenters for dealing with accelerator failures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jouppi et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> or for finding rings among accelerators at the start of an entire ML job <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khani et al., [n. d.]</a>; <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. These systems focus on coarse-grained reconfiguration that occurs either in case of failures or to optimize the initial topology for a specific job. In contrast, <span id="S1.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> focuses on fine-grained reconfiguration at the scale individual collectives to adapt to the communication patterns of collective algorithms. This difference in timescale and purpose leads to different design choices and challenges, as we will discuss in the paper.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15450v1/waveguides.svg" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:471/218;" width="471" height="218"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>. </span><span id="S1.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Waveguides + Mach-Zehnder interferometer (MZI) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2025</a>)</cite> switches on the interposer form a server-scale optical fabric.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Photonic scale-up GPU domains</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Many ML-centric cloud datacenters use optical circuit switches in the scale-out domain to interconnect racks of accelerators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jouppi et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. For example, the Google TPU datacenter uses optical circuit switches to interconnect racks of TPUs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jouppi et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Similarly, other cloud providers have replaced or augmented electrical leaf and spine switches of the Clos topology <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greenberg et al., 2009</a>)</cite> with optical circuit switches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Singh et al., 2015a</a>)</cite>. Note that these deployments leave the scale-up domains <em id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> datacenter racks and servers, as monolithic electrical blocks. For example, the NVIDIA DGX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nvidia DGX Systems, 2021</a>)</cite> GPU servers, NVL72 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">NVIDIA Corporation, 2024</a>)</cite> GPU racks or the Google TPU pods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jouppi et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, all use electrical interconnects to network GPUs within a server or a rack.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Photonic scale-up GPU domains.</span> 
Recently, the need for higher bandwidth and lower energy consumption in densely connected GPU racks has inspired architectures that introduce optical connectivity even into the scale-up domain, giving rise to <em id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">photonic GPU scale-up domains</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahni et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">HotChips 34, 5 26</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu and Bergman, 2025</a>)</cite>. Photonic connectivity in scale-up domains aims to replace some of the electrical interconnects within a server or a rack with optical interconnects. Several hardware architectures have been proposed to realize this vision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahni et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">HotChips 34, 5 26</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin et al., 2013</a>)</cite>. Some designs use co-packaged optical transponders to connect GPU servers to a silicon photonic switch, as proposed by Nvidia <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Corporation, [n. d.]</a>)</cite>, while others integrate chip-to-chipMZI
photonic connectivity directly into the server board using optical interposers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">HotChips 34, 5 26</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahni et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Chip-to-chip optical interconnects in GPU servers.</span> 
In this work, we propose to build scale-up domains with chip-to-chip photonic connectivity within GPU servers. Our design is enabled by newly viable optical interposers like <span id="S2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Passage</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. <span id="S2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Passage</span>, an optical interposer, is divided into <em id="S2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tiles</em> that form a grid. Electrical chips (<em id="S2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> GPUs, TPUs or XPUs) are bonded on top of the tiles of <span id="S2.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Passage</span>. These tiles connect to each other using an optical fabric consisting of waveguides and Mach-Zehnder interferometer (MZIs)-based optical switches. Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows the logical layout of the optical fabric on <span id="S2.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Passage</span>. The I/O (SerDes) ports of an electrical chip connect to transceiver (Tx/Rx) blocks available on <span id="S2.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Passage</span> tiles. The interconnect fabric on the interposer connects transceiver blocks to each other.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Programmable optical fabric.</span> 
A key feature of the optical interposer-based design is its use of <em id="S2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">programmable</em> optical switches in the fabric between GPUs (Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, bottom). These switches can be programmed to establish optical circuits between pairs of GPUs. This programmability enables us to adapt the interconnect to changing workloads and communication patterns. In contrast with packet-switched connections on state-of-the-art electrical scale-up interconnects (<em id="S2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> Nvlinks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nvidia NVLink, 2021</a>)</cite>, PCIe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Neugebauer et al., 2018</a>)</cite>), optical circuits eliminate contention for network resources on the path between two chips on the interposer. Recent work has shown that optical switches on the interposer can be reconfigured within 3.7 microseconds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, making it possible to quickly establish optical circuits between any pair of chips bonded on it.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Optical circuits in scale-up domains.</span> 
Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows an example of optical circuits constructed on the interposer by programming MZIs to the appropriate configuration. Specifically, this configuration makes two unidirectional circuits between tiles <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>: one from tiles 1 to 2 (blue) and vice versa (orange). The reticle stitch loss — loss incurred when the optical circuit crosses a tile — is <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.2</annotation></semantics></math> dB on average in the optical interposer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">HotChips 34, 5 26</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, allowing us to establish high-bandwidth optical circuits between GPUs. The optical interposer can support multiple such circuits simultaneously, enabling multiple pairs of GPUs to communicate at the same time without contention. This is achieved by the fabric consisting of thousands of waveguides since each waveguide is only a few micrometers wide.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cascading optical connectivity in the rack.</span> 
Similar to proposals in previous work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, servers with optical interposers connect to each other using fibers attached to the waveguides at the edge of a server as shown in (Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, right). This allows cascading multiple servers, each with its own optical interposer, to form a larger photonic scale-up domain as shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2. Photonic scale-up GPU domains ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Within each photonic scale-up domain, the programmable switches and low signal loss enable us to establish optical circuits between GPUs on different servers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">HotChips 34, 5 26</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. In such circuits, the optical signal originates at a tile transponder, traverses waveguides on the interposer, followed by optical fibers between servers and finally terminates at the transponder of the destination tile.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15450v1/pccl-passage.svg" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:310/156;" width="310" height="156"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>. </span><span id="S2.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Multiple servers cascaded to build a larger cluster.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Implications for distributed ML</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In distributed ML training and inference, intermediate parameters of the model at each accelerator are accumulated, reduced and transferred over the network between accelerators using <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">collective communication</em> primitives <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dongarra et al., 2013</a>)</cite> like <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> and <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllToAll</span>. This places collective communication on the critical path of both training and inference. Recently, researchers have shown that during model training, accelerators remain idle for large percentages of the time while waiting for inter-accelerator communication to complete <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">swi, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gebara et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lao et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, highlighting the importance of efficient collective communication.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Topology-aware collective algorithms.</span> 
Algorithms for collectives decide the order and amount of data exchanged between accelerators to implement a primitive. Their design has been an active area of research in high-performance computing (HPC) for decades <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dongarra et al., 2013</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thakur et al., 2005</a>)</cite>. Most of this research assumes the network between accelerators is ideal — that it has no topology constraints, no contention, and no bandwidth or latency variations at runtime. Recent work has shown that many of these algorithms perform worse than theoretical expectations in real-world deployments since they do not account for the topology and runtime characteristics of the interconnect between accelerators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shah et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cai et al., 2021a</a>)</cite>. A simple example is the use of the theoretically latency <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">and</em> bandwidth-optimal recursive halving and doubling (RHD) algorithm for <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thakur et al., 2005</a>)</cite>. Depending on the underlying interconnect topology, simultaneous transfers in RHD can overlap on the same network edge, leading to contention and poor performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sensi et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Cases like these have led researchers to develop topology-aware collective algorithms custom-fit to the underlying interconnect topology <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shah et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Won et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cai et al., 2021a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rashidi et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Our proposal: collective-aware network topologies.</span> 
In this work, we propose a radically different alternative: we adapt the interconnect topology to the communication patterns of known performant collective communication algorithms. This allows us to leverage decades of research in optimal collective algorithms and reconfigure the interconnect topology to match the communication patterns of these algorithms to avoid contention and bandwidth variations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>A playground for collective algorithms</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is tempting to believe that there exists a single “silver-bullet” collective algorithm that is optimal for a given collective operation across all scenarios. If this were true, we could simply construct fixed multi-accelerator topologies that match the assumptions of this algorithm and use it for all collective operations, obviating the need for any topology reconfiguration. However, this is not the case. Each collective algorithm has its strengths and weaknesses, and the choice of algorithm depends on the specific requirements of the collective in terms of data buffer size and the characteristics of the underlying hardware.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Selecting the right algorithm.</span> 
Algorithms are classified as <em id="S2.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">latency-optimal</em> or <em id="S2.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bandwidth-optimal</em> based on whether they minimize the latency or maximize the bandwidth of communication, respectively. The choice of algorithm depends both on the characteristics of the underlying interconnect, like its bandwidth and latency, and the size of the data buffer. For example, in a high-bandwidth network with high latency, a latency-optimal algorithm may be more efficient. Further, in data parallel <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span>, the amount of data transferred in each round can be very high due (<em id="S2.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> several GBs) to large model sizes, making bandwidth-optimal algorithms preferable. However, tensor parallel <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> in inference transfer smaller amounts of data (<em id="S2.SS2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> several MBs), making latency-optimal algorithms more preferable. Ideally, the collective communication library (CCL) should allow users to choose an optimal algorithm based on these characteristics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Matching topology to the algorithm.</span> 
A collective algorithm only achieves its theoretical performance underMZI
assumptions about the underlying interconnect topology. So, to truly enable a “choice” of algorithm, the network topology must adapt depending on the chosen algorithm; otherwise, the algorithm will be suboptimal due to runtime effects like congestion and dilation (§ <a href="#S3" title="3. Congestion + dilation in collectives ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). The programmability of photonic scale-up interconnects achieves this by creating on-demand, contention-free optical circuits between pairs of GPUs. This capability introduces a fundamentally richer set of algorithmic choices where communication algorithms can assume direct, contention-free connectivity between communicating GPUs — unlocking the use of optimal or near-optimal collective algorithms that otherwise underperform on fixed-topology electrical networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shah et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Won et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Photonic Collective Communication Library (<span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span>).</span> 
We introduce the Photonic Collective Communication Library (<span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span>), a novel collective communication library designed to leverage the dynamic topology reconfiguration capabilities of photonic interconnects in multi-GPU scale-up systems. <span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> unlocks an expansive landscape of algorithmic choices, enabling GPUs to communicate efficiently with dynamically changing sets of peers without incurring contention. By creating on-demand, contention-free optical circuits that precisely match the communication patterns of theoretically optimal collective algorithms, <span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> ensures high-performance collective operations without incurring congestion overheads at runtime. This flexibility bridges the longstanding gap between theoretical optimality and practical efficiency of collective algorithms, offering distributed ML programmers an unprecedented ability to select collective algorithms tailored to their workload. For example, we can create optical circuits to match the communication patterns of the RHD algorithm for <span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span>, eliminating network contention that would otherwise degrade performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.15450v1/ar.png" id="S2.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:338/124;" width="338" height="124" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>. </span><span id="S2.F4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">describes the <span id="S2.F4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> collective primitive.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Congestion + dilation in collectives</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A collective communication primitive has a <em id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pre-condition</em> and a <em id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">post-condition</em> that identifies the data chunks available at each GPU before and after the communication starts and ends, respectively. Figure <a href="#S2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 2.2. A playground for collective algorithms ‣ 2. Photonic scale-up GPU domains ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows the pre-condition and post-condition of the <span id="S3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> primitive. A collective communication algorithm defines the sequence of communication rounds between accelerators that achieve the post-condition of the collective. Each round consists of one or more data transfers between pairs of accelerators. The performance of a collective algorithm depends on the number of rounds, the amount of data transferred in each round, and the characteristics of the communication system.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We reason about the duration of a collective using the popular <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hockney, 1994</a>)</cite> cost model. Sending a data chunk incurs software overheads of preparing the memory buffer as well as the link latency (<em id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> propagation delay). Such costs are paid once for every transfer, independent of the size of the chunk. The <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> constant captures these fixed costs of sending a data chunk on a network link. The <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> costs are a function of the size of the buffer <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> as well as the bandwidth between the communicating accelerators. So, the cost of sending a chunk of size <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> from one accelerator to another is <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha+\beta\cdot d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha+\beta\cdot d</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\frac{1}{bandwidth}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\frac{1}{bandwidth}</annotation></semantics></math>. The goal of an optimal collective algorithm is to minimize the total <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha-\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha-\beta</annotation></semantics></math> cost.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-optimal vs. <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-optimal algorithms.</span> 
Decades of HPC research has developed a variety of algorithms for collective communication that optimize the <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha-\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha-\beta</annotation></semantics></math> costs. Often these algorithms optimize a specific aspect of the communication cost. Bandwidth optimality, or <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-optimality, means that each communicating node transfers the minimum possible amount of data required by the collective communication operation. For example, the Ring <span id="S3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> algorithm between <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> nodes minimizes the amount of data (<math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 2\cdot d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 2\cdot d</annotation></semantics></math>) transferred during the collective schedule, making it <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-optimal but suboptimal in <math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> cost, requiring <math id="S3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2(N-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2(N-1)</annotation></semantics></math> communication rounds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">NVIDIA, 2024</a>)</cite>. In contrast, the recursive halving and doubling (RHD) algorithm reduces the number of communication rounds to <math id="S3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\log n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\log n</annotation></semantics></math>, improving <math id="S3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> costs over the Ring algorithm while still being <math id="S3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-optimal when <math id="S3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is a power-of-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thakur et al., 2005</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15450v1/allgather-recursive-v2.svg" id="S3.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:333/175;" width="333" height="175"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>. </span><span id="S3.F5.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">shows the optimal communication schedule
for <span id="S3.F5.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllGather</span> where initially each GPU has one data chunk and the post condition requires that all GPUs have all data chunks (<math id="S3.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-8</annotation></semantics></math>). For conciseness, we annotate the arrows with the transfers from the left GPU to the right GPU only, omitting transfers in the reverse direction. This <span id="S3.F5.5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllGather</span> is the first half of the RHD <span id="S3.F5.5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span>. Note links with overlapping transfers (congested) are shown in red.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Difference between theory and practice</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Despite theoretical guarantees of optimality, the performance of collective communication algorithms in practice is often sub-optimal. In this section we identify the cause for the gap between the theoretical and practical performance of collective communication algorithms. Careful observation suggests that collective algorithms (<em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> Ring, RHD) assume that the network is ideal — each communicating node can send and receive data at the maximum bandwidth of the network link and within each round of communication, all data transfers take the same amount of time. In practice, both these assumptions can be violated due to phenomenon known as <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">congestion</em> and <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dilation</em> in the network <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Leighton and Maggs, 1995</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rothvoss, 2012b</a>)</cite>. We illustrate this with the RHD algorithm for <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Modeling congestion.</span> 
Consider an example where GPUs participating in an <span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> are connected physically in a ring topology, also known as a one-dimensional torus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jouppi et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Figure <a href="#S3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 3. Congestion + dilation in collectives ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows the communication schedule of the RHD algorithm for <span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> on the ring topology including data transfers in each round. Note that in some rounds, multiple data transfers overlap on the same network link. Overlapping transfers divide the bandwidth of the link among transfers causing <em id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">congestion</em> in the network. Due to this congestion, the effective bandwidth of the link is reduced, increasing the <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> cost of the communication. We model this as a congestion factor <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> that reduces the effective bandwidth of the link to <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{bandwidth}{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mi>c</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{bandwidth}{c}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of overlapping transfers on that link. We experimentally verify <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> by sharing the GPU2-GPU3 link on a 8 GPU machine across multiple transfers. Figure <a href="#S3.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 3.1. Difference between theory and practice ‣ 3. Congestion + dilation in collectives ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows that the bandwidth per transfer reduces by <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="4\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\times</annotation></semantics></math> when 2 and 4 transfers overlap on the GPU2-GPU3 link.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Modeling dilation.</span> 
In Figure <a href="#S3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 3. Congestion + dilation in collectives ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, communication paths used in each round of the RHD algorithm are not uniform. Some GPUs communicate on a 1-hop path, while others communicate over multiple hops. At each hop, the data needs to be stored and forwarded which introduces some latency. The longer the path length, the higher the latency of sending data between the GPUs. This is known as <em id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dilation</em> in the network. We model this as a dilation factor <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> as the number of hops between the source and destination GPU. The dilation factor <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> increases the effective latency of sending a chunk of size <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\cdot\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\cdot\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We extend the <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-<math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> model by introducing additional terms that capture the effects of congestion and dilation on the overall communication cost:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{communication\ cost}=\sum_{i=1}^{R}(c_{i}\cdot\beta\cdot d_{i}+d_{i}\cdot\alpha)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>communication cost</mtext><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>R</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{communication\ cost}=\sum_{i=1}^{R}(c_{i}\cdot\beta\cdot d_{i}+d_{i}\cdot\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of rounds in the collective communication algorithm, <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the congestion factor for round <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the dilation factor for round <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are the <em id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">maximum</em> values of <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> across all transfers in round <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, since <em id="S3.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">all</em> transfers must complete before round <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i{+}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i{+}1</annotation></semantics></math> begins.
Equation <a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1. Difference between theory and practice ‣ 3. Congestion + dilation in collectives ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> captures the total communication cost of a collective algorithm in practical environments, accounting for both congestion and dilation in the network at runtime. Even a known optimal algorithm’s performance can degrade significantly if the congestion and dilation factors are high.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15450v1/congestion.svg" id="S3.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:471/137;" width="471" height="137"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>. </span><span id="S3.F6.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Bandwidth between GPUs on different paths (<math id="S3.F6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GX=GPUX" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GX=GPUX</annotation></semantics></math>) on a H100 DGX. We copy a stream of 32 MB buffers between GPUs using <span id="S3.F6.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cudaMemCpyAsync</span>. We store and forward the buffers through GPUs to hop through them.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Circuit-switched collectives</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Theoretically optimal collective algorithms have shown limited utility in practice due to the gap between the assumptions of these algorithms and the reality of the underlying interconnects <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shah et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Won et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Programmable photonic interconnects provide a unique opportunity to bridge this gap by adapting the interconnect topology to the communication patterns of known performant collective algorithms. We discuss how <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> implements this vision to achieve optimal collective communication performance in practice.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Why can <span id="S4.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> eliminate congestion in collectives?</span> 
The key to eliminating congestion in <span id="S4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> is the use of optical circuits between GPUs. Unlike electrical packet-switched interconnects, which can suffer from congestion when data transfers overlap on the same network link, optical circuits provide contention-free connectivity. Each circuit is a dedicated path between two GPUs, allowing them to communicate without interference from other transfers. This means that all data transfers between GPUs in <span id="S4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> occur on direct optical circuits, eliminating congestion delays.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Why can <span id="S4.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> eliminate dilation in collectives?</span> 
Optical circuits on a photonic interconnect like <span id="S4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Passage</span> experience several forms of signal loss, including reticle-stitch loss (0.25 dB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2024</a>)</cite>), waveguide propagation loss (0.4 dB/cm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2024</a>)</cite>), and waveguide-to-fiber coupling loss (0.5 dB). To ensure uniform data rates across circuits of varying lengths, the launch power of the laser on the source tile (Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) is adjusted based on the length of the path—longer circuits that span more tiles or fibers are provisioned with higher launch power. As a result, circuits in the scale-up domain achieve uniform bandwidth regardless of path length. Thus, when GPUs communicate over optical circuits in the scale-up domain, regardless of the number of hops in the circuit, the data is never stored and forwarded on intermediate GPUs eliminating dilation. While light takes longer to travel on longer paths, the additional time is negligible since it is <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.3\mu{}s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.3\mu{}s</annotation></semantics></math> for every additional 100 meters.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Collective-aware topology design with <span id="S4.SS1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">So far we have proposed our vision: <em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">programming optical circuits between GPUs to match the communication patterns of established collective algorithms, thereby eliminating delays due to congestion and dilation</em>. This flexibility will allow selecting a collective algorithm for the specific characteristics of a workload and achieving its theoretical performance guarantee by adapting the interconnect topology to match the communication patterns of the algorithm. In this section we describe how <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> implements this vision in practice.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">To reconfigure or not to reconfigure?</span> 
<span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> considers whether or not to reconfigure the interconnect topology at the start of each round of communication in a collective algorithm, including the first round. The choice of whether to reconfigure the interconnect topology is complex. It depends on the trade-off between two opposing factors: (1) the latency of reconfiguring the interconnect and (2) the potential performance benefits from reconfiguration. As discussed in §<a href="#S2" title="2. Photonic scale-up GPU domains ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, programming photonic interconnects incurs a delay that depends on the hardware design. Reconfiguration delays can range from 3.7<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>s <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2024</a>)</cite> for <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Passage</span> to tens of milliseconds for MEMS-based circuit switches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jouppi et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Thus, <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> must carefully consider if the benefits of reconfiguration, measured by reduced congestion and dilation factors, outweigh the costs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Peeking into future rounds.</span> 
The choice of whether to reconfigure or not is made more complex by the need to make this decision in multiple rounds of the collective algorithm. For example, if <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> were to reconfigure before the first round of communication, it is entirely possible that the resulting reconfigured topology simply does not allow the communication in the second round of the algorithm (<em id="S4.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em>  the nodes communicating in the second round may not be connected in the reconfigured topology of the first round). Thus, <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> must also consider <em id="S4.SS1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">future</em> implications of reconfiguring in a given round. This requires <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> to have a global view of the collective algorithm and the communication patterns in all rounds of the algorithm.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Isn’t topology design intractable?</span> 
At first glance, <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span>’s task may seem intractable due to the vast search space of possible topologies and the complex dependencies between rounds. However, <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> sets up the algorithmic task such that it is not searching for arbitrary topologies by intelligently restricting it to a fixed set of topologies. This significantly reduces the search space and allows <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> to efficiently compute the optimal sequence of reconfigurations across multiple rounds of communication. Specifically, <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> formulates this as an optimization problem with the goal of achieving the lowest possible communication cost for a given collective algorithm.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> PCCL Algorithm</figcaption>
<p id="alg1.4" class="ltx_p ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom"><span id="alg1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inputs:</span> 
<br class="ltx_break">
<span id="alg1.4.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<span class="ltx_tbody">
<span id="alg1.4.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="alg1.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="alg1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> :</span>
<span id="alg1.4.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Initial topology</span></span>
<span id="alg1.4.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="alg1.4.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="alg1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\{G_{1},\ldots G_{s-1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\{G_{1},\ldots G_{s-1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span>
<span id="alg1.4.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Set of connected topologies</span></span>
<span id="alg1.4.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="alg1.4.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="alg1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\{R_{0},R_{1},\ldots,R_{n-1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\{R_{0},R_{1},\ldots,R_{n-1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span>
<span id="alg1.4.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Set of communication rounds</span></span>
<span id="alg1.4.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="alg1.4.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="alg1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}\in R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}\in R</annotation></semantics></math>:</span>
<span id="alg1.4.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Set of transfers between GPUs</span></span>
<span id="alg1.4.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="alg1.4.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row"></span>
<span id="alg1.4.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><math id="alg1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,d)</annotation></semantics></math> in round <math id="alg1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
<span id="alg1.4.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="alg1.4.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="alg1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I=\{G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I=\{G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="alg1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\forall i&gt;=s\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">&gt;</mo><mo lspace="0em">=</mo><mi>s</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall i&gt;=s\}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span>
<span id="alg1.4.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Topologies from connecting</span></span>
<span id="alg1.4.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="alg1.4.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row"></span>
<span id="alg1.4.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">pairs of GPUs in <math id="alg1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i-s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i-s}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
<span id="alg1.4.2.8" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="alg1.4.2.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="alg1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(S,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(S,I)</annotation></semantics></math> :</span>
<span id="alg1.4.2.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Set of all available topologies</span></span>
<span id="alg1.4.2.9" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="alg1.4.2.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="alg1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span>
<span id="alg1.4.2.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Transfer size for round <math id="alg1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
<span id="alg1.4.2.10" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="alg1.4.2.10.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="alg1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\beta</annotation></semantics></math>:</span>
<span id="alg1.4.2.10.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Cost coefficients</span></span>
<span id="alg1.4.2.11" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="alg1.4.2.11.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-bottom: 10.00002pt;"><math id="alg1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>:</span>
<span id="alg1.4.2.11.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-bottom: 10.00002pt;">Reconfiguration delay</span></span>
</span>
</span>
<span id="alg1.4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Outputs:
<br class="ltx_break">
<span id="alg1.4.3.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<span class="ltx_tbody">
<span id="alg1.4.3.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="alg1.4.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="alg1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.4.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">:</span></span>
<span id="alg1.4.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="alg1.4.3.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">Binary variable: 1 if round <math id="alg1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> uses</span></span></span>
<span id="alg1.4.3.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="alg1.4.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_td"></span>
<span id="alg1.4.3.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="alg1.4.3.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">topology <math id="alg1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j,\quad j\in[0\ldots i+|S|]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j,\quad j\in[0\ldots i+|S|]</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></span>
</span>
</span>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:10.0pt;"></span>
<span id="alg1.4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Minimize: </span> <math id="alg1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:10.0pt;">
<span id="alg1.4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">where:</span> 
<br class="ltx_break"><math id="alg1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}=\sum_{j=0}^{i+|S|}t_{i,j}\cdot\text{Cost}(G_{j},R_{i},w_{i},i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>Cost</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}=\sum_{j=0}^{i+|S|}t_{i,j}\cdot\text{Cost}(G_{j},R_{i},w_{i},i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> 
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.0pt;">
(<a href="#S4.E6" title="In 4.1. Collective-aware topology design with PCCL ‣ 4. Circuit-switched collectives ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) <math id="alg1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Cost}(G_{j},R_{i},w_{i},i,j)=\text{CommCost}(G_{j},R_{i},w_{i})+\text{ReconfCost}(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Cost</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>CommCost</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mtext>ReconfCost</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Cost}(G_{j},R_{i},w_{i},i,j)=\text{CommCost}(G_{j},R_{i},w_{i})+\text{ReconfCost}(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> 
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.0pt;">
<math id="alg1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{CommCost}(G_{j},R_{i},w_{i})=\text{Algorithm}\penalty\ \ref{alg:alphabeta}(G_{j},R_{i},w_{i},\alpha,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>CommCost</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Algorithm</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 4.1. Collective-aware topology design with PCCL ‣ 4. Circuit-switched collectives ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{CommCost}(G_{j},R_{i},w_{i})=\text{Algorithm}\penalty\ \ref{alg:alphabeta}(G_{j},R_{i},w_{i},\alpha,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> 
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.0pt;">
(<a href="#S4.E7" title="In 4.1. Collective-aware topology design with PCCL ‣ 4. Circuit-switched collectives ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) <math id="alg1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{ReconfCost}(i,j)=r\cdot\neg\left(\text{Bitmap}(t_{i,j})\land\text{Bitmap}(t_{i-1,j})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>ReconfCost</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>¬</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>Bitmap</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mtext>Bitmap</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{ReconfCost}(i,j)=r\cdot\neg\left(\text{Bitmap}(t_{i,j})\land\text{Bitmap}(t_{i-1,j})\right)</annotation></semantics></math> 
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:10.0pt;">
<span id="alg1.4.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">subject to</span>: 
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:2.8pt;">

<span id="alg1.4.7" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<span class="ltx_tbody">
<span id="alg1.4.7.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="alg1.4.7.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-bottom: 5.0pt;">(<a href="#S4.E4" title="In 4.1. Collective-aware topology design with PCCL ‣ 4. Circuit-switched collectives ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>)</span>
<span id="alg1.4.7.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-bottom: 5.0pt;"><math id="alg1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j=0}^{i+|S|}t_{i,j}=1,\quad\forall i\in\{0,1,\ldots,n-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j=0}^{i+|S|}t_{i,j}=1,\quad\forall i\in\{0,1,\ldots,n-1\}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span id="alg1.4.7.1.3" class="ltx_td" style="padding-bottom: 5.0pt;"></span></span>
<span id="alg1.4.7.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="alg1.4.7.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-bottom: 5.0pt;">(<a href="#S4.E5" title="In 4.1. Collective-aware topology design with PCCL ‣ 4. Circuit-switched collectives ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>)</span>
<span id="alg1.4.7.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-bottom: 5.0pt;"><math id="alg1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i,k}=1\Rightarrow t_{j,k}=1,\quad\forall k\geq|S|\quad\forall j\in[k,i-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i,k}=1\Rightarrow t_{j,k}=1,\quad\forall k\geq|S|\quad\forall j\in[k,i-1]</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span id="alg1.4.7.2.3" class="ltx_td" style="padding-bottom: 5.0pt;"></span></span>
</span>
</span></p>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inputs.</span> 
The algorithm requires multiple inputs. (1) An initial network topology <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the state of the scale-up topology before the collective communication starts. (2) A sequence of communication rounds <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R=\{R_{0},R_{1},\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\{R_{0},R_{1},\ldots</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext=",R_{n-1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">,R_{n-1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of data transfers <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,d)</annotation></semantics></math> that occur in round <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and their corresponding data sizes <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> defines a collective communication algorithm between <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> GPUs since it describes which GPUs transfer what data to each other in each communication round. Using <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> we derive the set <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> containing topologies resulting from connecting pairs of GPUs in each round’s transfers. (3) A candidate set <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> of standard connected topologies (<em id="S4.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> 2D mesh). We define <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> as a list of topologies where the first <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S|</annotation></semantics></math> topologies are from set <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and the remaining topologies are from <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>. Each topology <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}\in G</annotation></semantics></math>, is a logical topology where an edge represents a direct optical circuit between two GPUs. We describe how <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> constructs these circuits on the photonic scale-up domain in §<a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2. Creating circuits for PCCL ‣ 4. Circuit-switched collectives ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>. (4) Latency (<math id="S4.SS1.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>) and bandwidth (<math id="S4.SS1.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>) cost coefficients based on the underlying hardware, and (5) the reconfiguration delay, <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, of the photonic interconnect.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Key decision.</span> 
In each round, the ILP must decide to either reconfigure the topology and incur reconfigure delay or preserve the existing topology and incur congestion/dilation delays. To make the problem tractable, <span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> intelligently restricts the number of topology choices in each round.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Managing disconnected graphs.</span> 
However, restricting the topology choice in each round to only the one that exactly matches the current round’s transfer schedule could lead to suboptimal decision-making. The topology derived from communication patterns of a round (<math id="S4.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>)
may not connect the set of GPUs that communicate in the next round (<math id="S4.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math>), which would require many reconfigurations over subsequent rounds.  Our key insight is that when the reconfiguration cost is high, we can avoid disconnected graphs by reconfiguring to a standard connected graph (<em id="S4.SS1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> 2D mesh) and incur dilation and congestion costs in the subsequent rounds. <span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> allows for such optimizations by allowing the ILP to pick between (1) reconfiguring to the current round’s topology, (2) retaining the existing topology, and (3) reconfiguring to a standard connected graph in <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decision variables and objective.</span> 
The decision variable is <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math>, a binary indicator that equals 1 if round <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> uses topology <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>. Since each round is allowed to retain the existing topology, the round <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>’s topology can be any of the previous rounds’ topologies. Hence, <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[0\ldots|S|+i]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[0\ldots|S|+i]</annotation></semantics></math> indexes into the set of all the available topologies for the round: <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> from the ideal topologies for all previous rounds of the algorithm (including this one) and <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S|</annotation></semantics></math> from the set of standard connected graphs. The algorithm minimizes the total cost across all rounds:</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{Minimize: }\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}C_{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Minimize: </mtext><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>C</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Minimize: }\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p8.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the total cost for round <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, computed as</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}=\sum_{j=0}^{i+|S|}t_{i,j}\cdot\text{Cost}(G_{j},R_{i},w_{i},i,j)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>Cost</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}=\sum_{j=0}^{i+|S|}t_{i,j}\cdot\text{Cost}(G_{j},R_{i},w_{i},i,j)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p8.3" class="ltx_p">since only the cost of the topology selected for that round is actually incurred.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">One topology per round constraint.</span> 
This constraint ensures that for every round <math id="S4.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, the algorithm selects exactly one topology from the available options:</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j=0}^{i+|S|}t_{i,j}=1,\quad\forall i\in\{0,1,\ldots,n-1\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j=0}^{i+|S|}t_{i,j}=1,\quad\forall i\in\{0,1,\ldots,n-1\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Topology dependency constraint.</span> 
In each round, <span id="S4.SS1.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> chooses from a growing set of topologies, including both the predefined connected topologies in <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and the topologies derived from transfers in each round, until the current round. If a round uses a topology created from a previous round’s transfers, all intermediate rounds must also use the same topology because in each round <span id="S4.SS1.p10.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> is allowed only to reconfigure to the current round’s topology or a topology from the connected set <math id="S4.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i,k}=1\Rightarrow t_{j,k}=1,\quad\forall k\geq|S|\quad\forall j\in[k,i-1]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i,k}=1\Rightarrow t_{j,k}=1,\quad\forall k\geq|S|\quad\forall j\in[k,i-1]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cost computation.</span> 
The communication cost in round <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> has two parts: the cost of transferring data in the current round using topology <math id="S4.SS1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and the reconfiguration cost if the topology is changed from the previous round:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{CommCost}(G_{j},R_{i},w_{i})+\text{ReconfCost}(i,j)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>CommCost</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mtext>ReconfCost</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{CommCost}(G_{j},R_{i},w_{i})+\text{ReconfCost}(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">We compute the communication cost <math id="S4.SS1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{CommCost}(G_{j},R_{i},w_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>CommCost</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{CommCost}(G_{j},R_{i},w_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> using Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 4.1. Collective-aware topology design with PCCL ‣ 4. Circuit-switched collectives ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, which finds shortest paths for all transfers and calculates a cost based on dilation (longest path) and congestion (most used edge) as <math id="S4.SS1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\cdot\text{dilation}+\beta\cdot\text{congestion}\cdot w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>dilation</mtext></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>congestion</mtext><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\cdot\text{dilation}+\beta\cdot\text{congestion}\cdot w</annotation></semantics></math> on the current round’s network topology. The reconfiguration cost accounts for topology change:</p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{ReconfCost}(i,j)=r\cdot\neg\left(\text{Bitmap}(t_{i,j})\land\text{Bitmap}(t_{i-1,j})\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>ReconfCost</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>¬</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>Bitmap</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mtext>Bitmap</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{ReconfCost}(i,j)=r\cdot\neg\left(\text{Bitmap}(t_{i,j})\land\text{Bitmap}(t_{i-1,j})\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p13.2" class="ltx_p">This means the reconfiguration cost <math id="S4.SS1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is incurred only when switching from one topology to another between consecutive rounds. If the same topology is used in both round <math id="S4.SS1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i-1</annotation></semantics></math> and round <math id="S4.SS1.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, no reconfiguration cost is applied.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<div id="alg2.3" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{path\_lengths}\leftarrow[\,]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>path_lengths</mtext><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">[</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{path\_lengths}\leftarrow[\,]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{edge\_count}\leftarrow\{\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>edge_count</mtext><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{edge\_count}\leftarrow\{\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each transfer pair <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s_{i},d_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s_{i},d_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> shortest path <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> exists in <math id="alg1.l3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
               Add <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|P_{i}|-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|P_{i}|-1</annotation></semantics></math> to <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">path_lengths</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
               <span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|P_{i}|-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|P_{i}|-2</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
                 Let <span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">edge</span> <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="alg1.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P_{i}[j],P_{i}[j+1])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P_{i}[j],P_{i}[j+1])</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
                 Increment <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">edge_usage[edge]</span> by <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
               <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">else</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
              
               <span id="alg1.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> large penalty

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg1.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{dilation}\leftarrow\max(\textit{path\_lengths})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>dilation</mtext><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">path_lengths</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{dilation}\leftarrow\max(\textit{path\_lengths})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{congestion}\leftarrow\max(\textit{edge\_usage})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>congestion</mtext><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">edge_usage</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{congestion}\leftarrow\max(\textit{edge\_usage})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l15.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">15:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\cdot\text{dilation}+\beta\cdot\text{congestion}\cdot w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>dilation</mtext></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>congestion</mtext><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\cdot\text{dilation}+\beta\cdot\text{congestion}\cdot w</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> Compute Congestion and Dilation Cost</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">We note that both Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4.1. Collective-aware topology design with PCCL ‣ 4. Circuit-switched collectives ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 4.1. Collective-aware topology design with PCCL ‣ 4. Circuit-switched collectives ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> are run offline to generate the reconfiguration schedule for a given scale-up domain. Our evaluation shows both these algorithms converge in less than one second.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Creating circuits for <span id="S4.SS2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> requires that all circuits that underpin the connectivity of the chosen topology in a communication round can exist simultaneously. The number of outgoing circuits from a GPU can not exceed the number of optical transmitters (Tx); the number of incoming circuits can not exceed the number of optical receivers (Rx) available on the tile where the GPU is located. We divide the transmitters uniformly across all required connections by a GPU in a round. If the number of connections are higher, we split the round into multiple rounds until all the connections can be accommodated.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Every optical circuit connecting two GPUs needs to traverse the MZI mesh (Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) and also traverse fibers between the servers (Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2. Photonic scale-up GPU domains ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). We model the MZI mesh as a graph and develop an algorithm based on Dijkstra’s algorithm to find routes that do not overlap on any edge of this graph to preserve signal integrity. We describe this in Algorithm <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ Appendix B Circuits without overlaps ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> with further details in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Circuits without overlaps ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. We randomly sample pairs of waveguides on different edges of the mesh and compute routes for each pair using our algorithm. Figure <a href="#A2.F19.sf1" title="In Figure 19 ‣ B.1. Routing to minimize fiber requirement ‣ Appendix B Circuits without overlaps ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19(a)</span></a> shows that it finds routes in under 2.5 seconds on a <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256\times 256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256\times 256</annotation></semantics></math> grid with 65,000 MZIs, across different numbers of circuits passing through a server.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To connect servers in our design, we attach a fiber between waveguides on the edge of one server and those on the other server (Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2. Photonic scale-up GPU domains ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). Since there are 100s of waveguides within a server, it is operationally infeasible to connect servers by attaching fibers to all the waveguides at the edges. However, limiting this to a single fiber causes contention as there can only be one circuit of a given wavelength on a fiber. We model the server grid as a graph with servers as nodes and fibers as edges, assuming any circuit that enters a server can also exit it using Algorithm <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ Appendix B Circuits without overlaps ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. To route all the required circuits, we develop a flow conserving algorithm (Algorithm <a href="#alg4" title="Algorithm 4 ‣ B.1. Routing to minimize fiber requirement ‣ Appendix B Circuits without overlaps ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, Appendix <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1. Routing to minimize fiber requirement ‣ Appendix B Circuits without overlaps ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>) that minimizes overlaps on inter-server fibers. On a 64-server grid, the maximum number of fibers needed to support 100 and 512 random circuits is 7 and 31, respectively. The algorithm converged in under 10 seconds in all our experiments. Since communication in distributed ML is predictable and repetitive, we compute these routes offline and reuse across collective invocations.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Benchmarking <span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span></h2>

<figure id="S5.F7" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_center">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>. </span><span id="S5.F7.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">ReduceScatter<span id="S5.F7.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> performance across buffer sizes on 128 GPUs with a reconfiguration delay of <math id="S5.F7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\mu{}s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\mu{}s</annotation></semantics></math>. (HC=Grid)</span></span></figcaption><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15450v1/topo_comparison_128gpus_5micros_reconf.svg" id="S5.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/77;" width="476" height="77"></object>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F8" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_center">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>. </span><span id="S5.F8.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">ReduceScatter<span id="S5.F8.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> performance breakdown for 256 MB buffer on 128 GPUs with a reconfiguration delay of <math id="S5.F8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\mu{}s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\mu{}s</annotation></semantics></math>. (HC=Grid)</span></span></figcaption><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15450v1/cost_breakdown_128gpus_256MB_5micros_reconf.svg" id="S5.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/56;" width="476" height="56"></object>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F9" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_center">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F9.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 9</span>. </span><span id="S5.F9.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">ReduceScatter<span id="S5.F9.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> performance breakdown for 1 GB buffer on 128 GPUs with a reconfiguration delay of <math id="S5.F9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1ms" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1ms</annotation></semantics></math>. (HC=Grid)</span></span></figcaption><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15450v1/cost_breakdown_128gpus_1024MB_1000micros_reconf.svg" id="S5.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/62;" width="476" height="62"></object>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the performance of <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span>’s collectives in comparison to the optimal collective algorithms used in popular scale-up direct-connect topologies. These direct-connect topologies have been adopted to provide high in-rack bandwidth with lower power costs and improved scaling compared to switched topologies. We consider 5 baseline topologies: Ring, 2D Torus, 3D Torus, 2D Grid and 3D Grid. Grid is a torus without wrap around links. Many of these topologies are deployed in modern scale-up domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jouppi et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Won et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. <span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">For <span id="S5.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span>, these represent the starting topologies <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithms.</span> 
We compare to the hardest baselines: the corresponding known optimal <span id="S5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> algorithm for each topology, also used in practice in state-of-the-art ML scale-up domains. For example, Nvidia’s NCCL library <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">NVIDIA NCCL, 2021</a>)</cite> implements the bandwidth-optimal Ring algorithm for <span id="S5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ReduceScatter</span>. Similarly, Bucket is the known optimal algorithm for <span id="S5.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ReduceScatter</span> on the torus topologies deployed in Google’s TPU datacenters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jouppi et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Further, RHD is the bandwidth-optimal algorithm with the lowest known latency cost for <span id="S5.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ReduceScatter</span> when the number of GPUs is a power-of-2. We also compare with Swing, which improves RHD on direct-connect topologies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sensi et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL<span id="S5.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> Inputs.</span></span> 
As noted in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4.1. Collective-aware topology design with PCCL ‣ 4. Circuit-switched collectives ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <span id="S5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> requires a communication schedule for an algorithm as input. Due to its low latency and bandwidth costs, we provide the communication pattern of RHD as input (§<a href="#S3" title="3. Congestion + dilation in collectives ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). The interconnect topologies (<em id="S5.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> ring, torus) in <span id="S5.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span>’s case are initial configurations which it can adapt across rounds by incurring a reconfiguration delay. We set the reconfiguration delay to 5 <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>s, roughly the reconfiguration delay of photonic interconnects like <span id="S5.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Passage</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. We use <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=3\mu{}s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=3\mu{}s</annotation></semantics></math> based on latency measurements of NVIDIA’s H100 DGX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">NVIDIA Corporation, 2025</a>)</cite> and <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1/450" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>450</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1/450</annotation></semantics></math> s/GB since the H100 DGX supports 450 GB/s NVLinks. We evaluate <span id="S5.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ReduceScatter</span>, since <span id="S5.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> simply takes <math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times</annotation></semantics></math> the time of a <span id="S5.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ReduceScatter</span>, as it is a <span id="S5.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ReduceScatter</span> followed by a mirror-image <span id="S5.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllGather</span> and the two have the same cost <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sensi et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
We use the congestion/dilation factors (§<a href="#S3" title="3. Congestion + dilation in collectives ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) to calculate the extended <math id="S5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha-\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha-\beta</annotation></semantics></math> cost using Eq. <a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1. Difference between theory and practice ‣ 3. Congestion + dilation in collectives ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">ReduceScatter<span id="S5.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> performance comparison.</span></span> 
In Figure <a href="#S5.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 5. Benchmarking PCCL ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, we compare <span id="S5.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> to baseline algorithms while scaling the buffer size for each topology.
Across topologies, <span id="S5.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> outperforms several baselines, especially at larger buffer sizes on a scale-up domain of 128 GPUs, and matches the ideal algorithm for the topology. Critically, <span id="S5.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> is the only system that is optimal on all the topologies. All other algorithms incur congestion or dilation on topologies that are not ideal for the algorithm.
Figures <a href="#A2.F17" title="Figure 17 ‣ Appendix B Circuits without overlaps ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a> and  <a href="#A2.F18" title="Figure 18 ‣ Appendix B Circuits without overlaps ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a> in the Appendix show the performance of <span id="S5.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> on scale-up domains of 64 and 32 GPUs, respectively. The performance trends are similar to those on 128 GPUs, with <span id="S5.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> outperforming all baselines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Communication cost breakdown.</span> 
We unpack why <span id="S5.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> outperforms baselines by analyzing the communication-time breakdown of each algorithm.
Figures <a href="#S5.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 5. Benchmarking PCCL ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> and <a href="#S5.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 5. Benchmarking PCCL ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> show this breakdown for 128 GPUs with 256 MB and 1 GB buffer sizes and <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\mu{}s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\mu{}s</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1ms" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1ms</annotation></semantics></math> reconfiguration delays respectively. While baselines suffer from higher dilation delays with the smaller 256 MB buffer, they suffer more from congestion delays with the larger 1 GB buffer. <span id="S5.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> continues to match the best algorithm for each topology when the reconfiguration delay is <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\mu s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\mu s</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, <span id="S5.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> is the only algorithm that achieves optimal performance on 2D and 3D grids since they are not an ideal topological fit for any baseline algorithm.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">AllToAll<span id="S5.p6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> performance.</span></span> 
Scale-up domains must also exhibit strong <span id="S5.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllToAll</span> performance due to expert parallelism in MoE models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shazeer et al., 2017</a>)</cite>. Thus, we compare <span id="S5.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> to the direct-exchange hypercube <span id="S5.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllToAll</span> algorithm (DEX) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Foster, 1995</a>)</cite>, a latency-optimal algorithm with <math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log_{2}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log_{2}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> steps suitable for the latency-sensitive <span id="S5.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllToAll</span>’s in MoE models.  As is standard, we feed DEX’s schedule as input to <span id="S5.p6.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span>. Figure <a href="#S5.F10.sf1" title="In Figure 10 ‣ 5. Benchmarking PCCL ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10(a)</span></a> shows that <span id="S5.p6.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> outperforms DEX across all the topologies, since none of the topologies are an ideal fit for this algorithm. Our evaluation indicates the <span id="S5.p6.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> outperforms baselines for both <span id="S5.p6.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> <em id="S5.p6.1.10" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">and</em> <span id="S5.p6.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllToAll</span>, cementing its optimality and flexibility across use cases.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F10" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F10.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15450v1/all_to_all_breakdown_128gpus_32MB_5micros_reconf.svg" id="S5.F10.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/92;" width="476" height="92"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F10.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S5.F10.sf1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="font-size:90%;">AllToAll<span id="S5.F10.sf1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F10.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15450v1/cdf.svg" id="S5.F10.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/238;" width="476" height="238"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F10.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S5.F10.sf2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">All-reduce buffer sizes.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F10.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 10</span>. </span><span id="S5.F10.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">shows performance breakdown for 32 MB buffer on 128 GPUs with a reconfiguration delay of <math id="S5.F10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\mu s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\mu s</annotation></semantics></math> (HC=Grid). Figure  <a href="#S5.F10.sf2" title="In Figure 10 ‣ 5. Benchmarking PCCL ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10(b)</span></a> shows the breakdown of the buffer sizes used in <span id="S5.F10.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> calls in Bert.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Why does <span id="S5.p7.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> outperform the baselines?</span> 
In Figure <a href="#S5.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 5. Benchmarking PCCL ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, where the reconfiguration cost is <math id="S5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\mu s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\mu s</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S5.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> achieves the best possible performance on all topologies by adapting the topology to the communication pattern of each round, thereby eliminating congestion and dilation costs. The smaller per-round reconfiguration cost incurred by <span id="S5.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> in this setting is offset by the savings from eliminating congestion and dilation costs. In contrast, all baseline algorithms incur significant congestion and dilation costs when the topology is not an ideal fit.
For example, the 3D-bucket algorithm is optimal on a 3D torus topology since it does not incur any congestion or dilation costs. However, it performs significantly worse on other topologies. <span id="S5.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span>’s adaptability becomes even more evident in Figure <a href="#S5.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 5. Benchmarking PCCL ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, where the buffer size is larger and the cost of incurring congestion is higher at 1 millisecond (Equation <a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1. Difference between theory and practice ‣ 3. Congestion + dilation in collectives ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). In such cases, <span id="S5.p7.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> can evaluate the tradeoff between reconfiguration costs and congestion/dilation costs and choose whether to adapt the topology accordingly. In Figure <a href="#S5.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 5. Benchmarking PCCL ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, <span id="S5.p7.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> chooses to reconfigure only 4 times, instead incurring congestion and dilation, due to the higher cost of reconfiguration. This contrasts with Figure <a href="#S5.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 5. Benchmarking PCCL ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, where <span id="S5.p7.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> reconfigures <math id="S5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log_{2}(128)=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>128</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log_{2}(128)=7</annotation></semantics></math> times.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.p8.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="143.23" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 143.23" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,143.23) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 12.92 L 0 130.31 C 0 137.45 5.78 143.23 12.92 143.23 L 464.46 143.23 C 471.59 143.23 477.38 137.45 477.38 130.31 L 477.38 12.92 C 477.38 5.78 471.59 0 464.46 0 L 12.92 0 C 5.78 0 0 5.78 0 12.92 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.11 12.92 L 1.11 130.31 C 1.11 136.84 6.39 142.12 12.92 142.12 L 464.46 142.12 C 470.98 142.12 476.27 136.84 476.27 130.31 L 476.27 12.92 C 476.27 6.39 470.98 1.11 464.46 1.11 L 12.92 1.11 C 6.39 1.11 1.11 6.39 1.11 12.92 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 10.58 99.83)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:32.97em;--ltx-fo-height:2.77em;--ltx-fo-depth:6.85em;font-size:10pt;" height="133.14" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 38.36)" width="456.21"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S5.p8.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:32.97em;">
<span id="S5.p8.pic1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="S5.p8.pic1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p8.pic1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Key Takeaways:</span></span>
<span id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<span id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">PCCL</span><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> achieves near theoretical performance of multiple collectives irrespective of input topology.</span></span>
</span></span>
<span id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">PCCL</span><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> intelligently trades off reconfiguration costs with congestion/dilation costs.</span></span>
</span></span>
</span>
</span></span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>End-to-end evaluation</h2>

<figure id="S6.F11" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15450v1/taskgraph.svg" id="S6.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/180;" width="381" height="180"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F11.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 11</span>. </span><span id="S6.F11.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Directed graph of a model’s iteration. (G = GPU)</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we evaluate <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span>’s performance on training transformer models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Devlin et al., 2018</a>)</cite> using FlexFlow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jia et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Unger et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. FlexFlow is the state of the art ML training simulator used to evaluate ML clusters with reconfigurable optical networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. FlexFlow models distributed training iteration as a task graph (shown in Figure <a href="#S6.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ 6. End-to-end evaluation ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) containing compute nodes and communication nodes connected by edges that denote data dependencies. It simulates the end-to-end runtime by iterating through the graph in a topologically sorted order. Prior work using FlexFlow to evaluate optical networks first generates an optimal parallelization strategy using graph optimizations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> on the task graph. We retain the same setup and generate an optimal parallelization strategy first and then replace the values of communication nodes with different algorithms to generate associated end-to-end iteration times.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F12" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_center">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F12.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 12</span>. </span><span id="S6.F12.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Training throughput of Bert Transformer on 32, 64 and 128 GPUs (HC=Grid). With <math id="S6.F12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\mu{}s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\mu{}s</annotation></semantics></math> reconfiguration delay, <span id="S6.F12.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> matches the performance of the ideal algorithms on topologies with an ideal <span id="S6.F12.5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> algorithm (Ring, 2D, 3D Torus). <span id="S6.F12.5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> outperforms all the algorithms on topologies without an ideal algorithm (2D, 3D HC).</span></figcaption><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15450v1/bert_combined_relative_throughput_comparison.svg" id="S6.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/78;" width="476" height="78"></object>
</figure>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Modeling communication nodes.</span> 
The communication in distributed ML training can be broadly classified into two types: <span id="S6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Peer-to-peer</span> and collectives:</p>
<ul id="S6.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Peer-to-peer</span> traffic arises when a model layer’s output shard is consumed by the next layer which is on a different GPU, <em id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> in pipeline parallelism. This communication pattern may not fit the pattern of a standard collective.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Collectives (<em id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> <span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span>, <span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllToAll</span>) are known communication patterns generated by distributed training. For example, data parallelism and tensor parallelism induce <span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> whereas expert parallelism induces <span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllToAll</span>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S6.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We tag each communication node in the task graph as one of the above by parsing the communication pattern. For collectives, we feed a collective algorithm schedule to <span id="S6.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> which reconfigures the topology to optimize collective runtime. For <span id="S6.p2.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Peer-to-peer</span>, <span id="S6.p2.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> generates point-to-point circuits.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Co-scheduling communication operations with <span id="S6.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span>.</span> 
A parallelization strategy can force a layer to enqueue <span id="S6.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Peer-to-peer</span> and <span id="S6.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> at nearly the same time, <em id="S6.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> during a backward pass with data and pipeline parallelism. <span id="S6.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> greedily prioritizes <span id="S6.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Peer-to-peer</span> transfers since they are always on the critical path and cannot be overlapped, whereas <span id="S6.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> could, in some cases, be partially overlapped. <span id="S6.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> implements this within FlexFlow by adding a dependency edge from the <span id="S6.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Peer-to-peer</span> to <span id="S6.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> node.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Accuracy of large-scale simulation.</span> 
FlexFlow simulates training on a larger number of GPUs by using a single GPU. It calculates the runtime of compute nodes by executing individual shards of layers assigned to compute nodes on the real GPU. FlexFlow also exposes an interface to calculate communication node costs that integrates with analytical network models like the <math id="S6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{-}\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{-}\beta</annotation></semantics></math> model or various network simulators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. In <span id="S6.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span>, we use the <math id="S6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha-\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha-\beta</annotation></semantics></math> model—the standard model also used in state-of-the-art distributed ML simulators such as Astra-Sim <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Won et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and SimAI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>—along with congestion and dilation delays, to model the network. Using real execution times for computation nodes along with a well-established, empirically validated model of the network enables accurate simulation of the total execution time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Workload.</span> 
We simulate the training of a Transformer-based model (12 layers, 16 attention heads, 2048 hidden dimension) across cluster sizes of 32, 64 and 128 GPUs. We execute the FlexFlow simulation on a single A100 GPU with 80 GB of memory. We set the batch size to 16 per GPU and maximum sequence length to 64 tokens. We generate an optimized parallelization strategy to shard the model across all the available GPUs using FlexFlow and then assign different values to the communication nodes corresponding to the collective algorithm and the topology. We set the reconfiguration delay to <math id="S6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\mu{}s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\mu{}s</annotation></semantics></math>, modeling the state of the art hardware <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL<span id="S6.p6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> Inputs</span></span> 
We use the inputs described in § <a href="#S5" title="5. Benchmarking PCCL ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> for <span id="S6.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span>. To determine the input schedule for <span id="S6.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span>, we profiled the buffer sizes of the <span id="S6.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> nodes in the transformer’s training taskgraph. Figure <a href="#S5.F10.sf2" title="In Figure 10 ‣ 5. Benchmarking PCCL ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10(b)</span></a> shows that the profiled <span id="S6.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> buffer sizes range from 1 MB which is latency sensitive to 64 MB which is bandwidth sensitive. We pick the latency and bandwidth optimal RHD schedule as the input to <span id="S6.p6.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> to achieve the optimal <span id="S6.p6.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> time across buffer sizes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span> 
We compare <span id="S6.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> with the same baselines from §<a href="#S5" title="5. Benchmarking PCCL ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. TopoOpt generates ring topologies to reduce congestion across different collective calls. Comparing <span id="S6.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> with ring algorithm on its ideal topology fit – ring topology, also serves as the best case comparison with TopoOpt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">How does <span id="S6.p8.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> impact ML training?</span> 
<span id="S6.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span>’s performance converges to the best performance on every topology we evaluated and matches the algorithm that is an ideal fit for the topology (<em id="S6.p8.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> 2D bucket on 2D mesh and 3D bucket on 3D torus). Interestingly, <span id="S6.p8.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> outperforms the ideal algorithm on the ring topology by reducing the <math id="S6.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> cost from linear to logarithmic in the number of GPUs. The extra reconfiguration cost incurred by <span id="S6.p8.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> is offset by the improvement in <math id="S6.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> cost. Swing’s <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sensi et al., 2024</a>)</cite> performance trend is similar to <span id="S6.p8.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> in matching the ideal algorithm on ring, 2D and 3D torus because it incurs a bounded congestion of <math id="S6.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="19\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>19</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">19\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\%</annotation></semantics></math> on the 2D and 3D torus respectively. However, <span id="S6.p8.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> outperforms all the algorithms including Swing on topologies without an ideal <span id="S6.p8.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AllReduce</span> algorithm (2D, 3D grid) by up to <math id="S6.p8.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1.2\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.2\times</annotation></semantics></math> since <span id="S6.p8.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> dynamically creates optical circuits to match the algorithm’s communication patterns.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">How does <span id="S6.p9.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> handle different reconfiguration delays?</span> 
We simulate the same workload across 32,64 and 128 GPUs with 10, 25, 50 and <math id="S6.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="500\mu{}s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>500</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">500\mu{}s</annotation></semantics></math> as the reconfiguration delay. Figures <a href="#A1.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ Appendix A End-to-end evaluation experiments. ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>- <a href="#A1.F16" title="Figure 16 ‣ Appendix A End-to-end evaluation experiments. ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a> in the Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A End-to-end evaluation experiments. ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> show that at lower reconfiguration delays, <span id="S6.p9.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> shows similar performance trends as Figure <a href="#S6.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ 6. End-to-end evaluation ‣ PCCL: Photonic circuit-switched collective communication for distributed ML" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>. At higher reconfiguration delays (<math id="S6.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="500\mu{}s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>500</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">500\mu{}s</annotation></semantics></math>), instead of reconfiguring in every round, <span id="S6.p9.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> reduces the reconfiguration and incurs congestion and dilation delays. Thus, <span id="S6.p9.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PCCL</span> adapts to the reconfiguration delay of the photonic hardware, choosing to reconfigure when the benefits (<em id="S6.p9.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> improved <math id="S6.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha-\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha-\beta</annotation></semantics></math> costs due to a superior algorithm like RHD, and lack of congestion/dilation from contention-free optical circuits) outweigh the costs (<em id="S6.p9.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> reconfiguration delay).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datacenter-scale optics.</span> 
Google recently replaced spine layer
packet switches in their datacenter with
optical circuit switches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jouppi et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Singh et al., 2015b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Singh et al., 2015a</a>)</cite>. Researchers have also used optics for reconfiguring
the datacenter interconnect <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mellette et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hamedazimi et al., 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Teh et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
<span id="S7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Silicon photonics for ML.</span><span id="S7.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> 
Recent work has began to use silicon photonic components for machine
learning workloads </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S7.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khani et al., [n. d.]</a>; <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin et al., 2013</a><span id="S7.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S7.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Systems like TopoOpt </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S7.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a><span id="S7.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S7.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
and SipML, use the ring algorithm and reconfigure only once per job.
TopoOpt finds ring topologies to minimize congestion across collective groups.
SipML</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S7.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khani et al., [n. d.]</a><span id="S7.p2.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S7.p2.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> argues that the reconfiguration cost is justified only
if the new topology is used for a significant fraction
of the total circuit establishment and utilization time.
In contrast, we show that active reconfiguration within a collective
results in end-to-end gains even if
the utilization time is not significant. In
</span><span id="S7.p2.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">PCCL</span><span id="S7.p2.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, each GPU sends </span><math id="S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="log_{2}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">log_{2}(N)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.p2.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> data chunks
compared to </span><math id="S7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.p2.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> chunks in the ring algorithm while retaining the same </span><math id="S7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.p2.1.19" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> cost.
Incurring reconfiguration delay </span><math id="S7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="log_{2}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">log_{2}(N)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.p2.1.20" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> times is cheaper than incurring </span><math id="S7.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\cdot\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" lspace="0.222em" mathcolor="#000000" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\cdot\alpha</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.p2.1.21" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
when </span><math id="S7.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.p2.1.22" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and reconfiguration delay are comparable.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Synthesizing collectives.</span><span id="S7.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> 
Researchers have proposed several algorithms that achieve the theoretical lower bound on the time taken by collectives </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S7.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thakur et al., 2005</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Leighton et al., 1994</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rothvoss, 2012a</a><span id="S7.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S7.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Recent work </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S7.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cai et al., 2021b</a><span id="S7.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S7.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> has shown that synthesis is a promising approach for generating collective algorithms for different topologies. However, scaling these approaches to multi-server accelerator topologies has been a challenge. We found that the synthesis time for </span><span id="S7.p3.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">AllGather</span><span id="S7.p3.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><span id="S7.p3.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">AllToAll</span><span id="S7.p3.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> collectives on topologies of two Azure NDv2 nodes and two Nvidia DGX2 nodes using SCCL </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S7.p3.1.15" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cai et al., 2021b</a><span id="S7.p3.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S7.p3.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> exceeds 24 hours </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S7.p3.1.19" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shah et al., 2023</a><span id="S7.p3.1.21" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S7.p3.1.22" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S7.p3.1.23" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Behnaz Arzani, 2023</a><span id="S7.p3.1.25" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S7.p3.1.26" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> improve on previous work using traffic engineering techniques.
Recent efforts achieve efficient collective communication on heterogeneous
</span><em id="S7.p3.1.27" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">networks of workstations</em><span id="S7.p3.1.28" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S7.p3.1.29" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Banikazemi et al., 1998</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhat and Prasanna, 2003</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lowekamp and Beguelin, 1996</a>; <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gong et al., 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fei et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li and Hoefler, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hoefler et al., 2010</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Opt, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pat, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Net, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Col, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">COO, 2023</a><span id="S7.p3.1.31" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S7.p3.1.32" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
Most recently, authors in </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S7.p3.1.33" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025b</a><span id="S7.p3.1.35" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S7.p3.1.36" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> synthesize efficient direct connect topologies
for collectives in distributed ML. This work targets the scale-out domain
and evaluates on scale-out network speeds of 100 Gbps while </span><span id="S7.p3.1.37" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">PCCL</span><span id="S7.p3.1.38" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is targeting
scale-up domains with an order of magnitude higher bandwidth and lower latency.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
